From: Gemini PDA Linux port <mratan/gemini-linux>
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: write the five that were missing

The #40 audit listed seven compatibles that have a driver in this tree but
no binding.  These are five of them, plus the two schema changes the
remaining two need.

  * regulator/mediatek,mt6797-cpu-sram-ldo -- VSRAM for the A53 clusters
    is eight LDOs inside INFRACFG_AO, not a PMIC rail, and cpufreq cannot
    reach the top of either OPP table until it is raised.
  * input/touchscreen/novatek,nt36xxx-ts -- a TDDI part, so the panel
    phandle is what makes it a drm_panel_follower.
  * usb/onsemi,fusb301a -- CC logic, no PD.  Two per board here.
  * display/panel/planet,gemini-panel -- deliberately names the machine
    and not the panel: two different panels shipped and only the
    bootloader knows which one is fitted.
  * net/wireless/mediatek,mt6797-consys -- documented so the tree checks,
    and marked NOT FOR UPSTREAM in its own description.  It covers the
    consys/wifi/btif nodes together because the vendor stack finds three
    of them with of_find_compatible_node() instead of binding to them.
  * usb/mediatek,musb gains mediatek,mt6797-musb: the musbfsh variant,
    which stands alone rather than falling back to mediatek,mtk-musb
    (that config enumerates but never passes bulk on this SoC) and has
    one clock rather than three.
  * vendor-prefixes gains "onsemi".

A correction to that audit while here: it called the three btif* nodes
the only genuine deletion candidates.  They are not.  mtk_btif is bound
to 1100c000.btif on the running device and mtk_stp_wmt_soc depends on it;
btif_tx and btif_rx never get a driver but btif_dma_plat.c reads their
reg and interrupts via of_find_compatible_node().  Deleting any of the
three breaks the Wi-Fi that ADR-0001 closed.

Not covered, and left for a submission that carries the drivers:
mediatek,mt6797-musb's driver change itself, and gemini,mt6797-consys-wmt
/ mediatek,wifi, which should not be in an upstream dtsi at all.

---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/regulator/mediatek,mt6797-cpu-sram-ldo.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/regulator/mediatek,mt6797-cpu-sram-ldo.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: MediaTek MT6797 CPU SRAM LDO
+
+maintainers:
+  - Gemini PDA Linux port <https://github.com/mratan/gemini-linux>
+
+description: |
+  VSRAM for the MT6797's Cortex-A53 clusters is not a PMIC rail. It is an array
+  of eight LDOs inside INFRACFG_AO, programmed through CPULDO_CTRL_0/1/2 at
+  offsets 0xf98, 0xf9c and 0xfa0.
+
+  It matters because the SRAM supply has to sit at or above Vproc: cpufreq
+  cannot take either cluster to the top of its OPP table until this has been
+  raised first. Expressing it as a regulator is what lets the OPP core sequence
+  the two together.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: regulator.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: mediatek,mt6797-cpu-sram-ldo
+
+  mediatek,infracfg:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/phandle
+    description:
+      A phandle to the INFRACFG_AO syscon holding the CPULDO_CTRL registers.
+      This must be the always-on block at 0x10001000; the second infracfg block
+      reads back as zero for these registers.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - mediatek,infracfg
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    regulator-cpu-sram {
+        compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-cpu-sram-ldo";
+        mediatek,infracfg = <&infracfg_ao>;
+        regulator-name = "vsram_cpu";
+        regulator-min-microvolt = <900000>;
+        regulator-max-microvolt = <1150000>;
+    };
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/input/touchscreen/novatek,nt36xxx-ts.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/input/touchscreen/novatek,nt36xxx-ts.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Novatek NT36xxx touchscreen controller
+
+maintainers:
+  - Gemini PDA Linux port <https://github.com/mratan/gemini-linux>
+
+description: |
+  A capacitive touch controller found in the NT36672 family. On the parts this
+  binding was written against it is a TDDI device: touch and display share one
+  die and one power domain, so the panel's prepare/unprepare IS the touch
+  controller's power sequence. Naming the panel makes the driver a
+  drm_panel_follower, which is how it learns not to poll an unpowered chip and
+  how to re-initialise one that has just come back from cold.
+
+  The chip answers at two addresses. "reg" is the control address; point reports
+  are read from address 0x01, which the driver derives itself.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: touchscreen.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: novatek,nt36xxx-ts
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  interrupts:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  irq-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      The controller's data-ready line as a plain GPIO, for a host whose
+      interrupt controller cannot take it. The driver polls and uses this line
+      to gate the read -- without that gate the point buffer reads as junk.
+      Supply this or "interrupts".
+
+  reset-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  panel:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/phandle
+    description:
+      The panel this controller shares its die and power domain with. Required
+      on a TDDI part; meaningless on a discrete one.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+
+anyOf:
+  - required: [interrupts]
+  - required: [irq-gpios]
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
+    i2c {
+        #address-cells = <1>;
+        #size-cells = <0>;
+
+        touchscreen@62 {
+            compatible = "novatek,nt36xxx-ts";
+            reg = <0x62>;
+            reset-gpios = <&pio 68 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+            irq-gpios = <&pio 85 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+            panel = <&panel>;
+            touchscreen-swapped-x-y;
+            touchscreen-inverted-y;
+        };
+    };
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/onsemi,fusb301a.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/usb/onsemi,fusb301a.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: ON Semiconductor FUSB301A USB Type-C controller
+
+maintainers:
+  - Gemini PDA Linux port <https://github.com/mratan/gemini-linux>
+
+description: |
+  A Type-C CC-logic controller: it detects attach, resolves cable orientation
+  and reports the advertised current, but does no power delivery.
+
+  A board may fit more than one, on different buses, one per port.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: onsemi,fusb301a
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  interrupts:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      The INT_N pin. Optional -- the driver polls when it is absent, which is
+      what a host with no usable interrupt controller for this line must do.
+
+  vbus-supply:
+    description:
+      The VBUS source this controller switches when acting as a source.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    i2c {
+        #address-cells = <1>;
+        #size-cells = <0>;
+
+        typec@25 {
+            compatible = "onsemi,fusb301a";
+            reg = <0x25>;
+            vbus-supply = <&otg_vbus>;
+        };
+    };
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/display/panel/planet,gemini-panel.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/display/panel/planet,gemini-panel.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Planet Computers Gemini PDA DSI panel
+
+maintainers:
+  - Gemini PDA Linux port <https://github.com/mratan/gemini-linux>
+
+description: |
+  The 5.99" 1080x2160 DSI panel of the Planet Computers Gemini PDA.
+
+  There is deliberately no panel-specific compatible here. Two different panels
+  were fitted across production -- a Novatek NT36672 and a Solomon SSD2092 --
+  and the board cannot tell you which one it has. The bootloader can: it probes
+  the panel at every boot and publishes the name in /chosen/atag,videolfb, which
+  is where the driver reads it from. So the compatible names the machine, and
+  the driver resolves the part at runtime.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: panel-common.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: planet,gemini-panel
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  avdd-supply:
+    description: Positive analog supply, typically +5.5 V.
+
+  avee-supply:
+    description: Negative analog supply, typically -5.5 V.
+
+  reset-gpios: true
+  backlight: true
+  port: true
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - avdd-supply
+  - avee-supply
+  - reset-gpios
+  - port
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
+    dsi {
+        #address-cells = <1>;
+        #size-cells = <0>;
+
+        panel@0 {
+            compatible = "planet,gemini-panel";
+            reg = <0>;
+            avdd-supply = <&lcd_avdd>;
+            avee-supply = <&lcd_avee>;
+            reset-gpios = <&pio 180 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+            backlight = <&backlight_lcd>;
+
+            port {
+                panel_in: endpoint {
+                    remote-endpoint = <&dsi_out>;
+                };
+            };
+        };
+    };
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/net/wireless/mediatek,mt6797-consys.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/net/wireless/mediatek,mt6797-consys.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: MediaTek MT6797 CONSYS connectivity subsystem
+
+maintainers:
+  - Gemini PDA Linux port <https://github.com/mratan/gemini-linux>
+
+description: |
+  NOT PROPOSED FOR UPSTREAM. This documents the device tree an out-of-tree
+  vendor connectivity stack requires, so that a tree carrying that stack can
+  still pass dt_binding_check. Anything submitting the MT6797 upstream should
+  leave these nodes out of the SoC dtsi entirely and carry them in a downstream
+  overlay, because there is no mainline driver for any of them and the
+  compatible strings are not SoC-qualified in the way a reviewer would ask for.
+
+  CONSYS is the MT6797's combo connectivity block: a separate MCU with its own
+  power domain, running firmware loaded by the AP, that terminates Wi-Fi,
+  Bluetooth, GPS and FM. The AP talks to it over BTIF, a dedicated FIFO with
+  its own APDMA channels.
+
+  The nodes are separate because the vendor stack finds three of them with
+  of_find_compatible_node() rather than by binding a driver to them.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    enum:
+      - gemini,mt6797-consys-wmt
+      - mediatek,wifi
+      - mediatek,btif
+      - mediatek,btif_tx
+      - mediatek,btif_rx
+
+  reg:
+    minItems: 1
+    maxItems: 4
+
+  interrupts:
+    minItems: 1
+    maxItems: 2
+
+  clocks:
+    minItems: 1
+    maxItems: 2
+
+  clock-names:
+    minItems: 1
+    maxItems: 2
+
+  power-domains:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  memory-region:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  hardware-values:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32-array
+    description:
+      Opaque tuning words handed to the Wi-Fi firmware verbatim.
+
+  vcn18-supply:
+    description: 1.8 V CONSYS supply.
+  vcn28-supply:
+    description: 2.8 V CONSYS supply.
+  vcn33_bt-supply:
+    description: 3.3 V Bluetooth supply.
+  vcn33_wifi-supply:
+    description: 3.3 V Wi-Fi supply.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/arm-gic.h>
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
+
+    btif@1100c000 {
+        compatible = "mediatek,btif";
+        reg = <0x1100c000 0x1000>;
+        interrupts = <GIC_SPI 130 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
+        clocks = <&infracfg 1>, <&infracfg 2>;
+        clock-names = "btifc", "apdmac";
+    };
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/mediatek,musb.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/mediatek,musb.yaml
@@ -15,12 +15,14 @@
     pattern: '^usb@[0-9a-f]+$'
 
   compatible:
-    items:
-      - enum:
-          - mediatek,mt8516-musb
-          - mediatek,mt2701-musb
-          - mediatek,mt7623-musb
-      - const: mediatek,mtk-musb
+    oneOf:
+      - items:
+          - enum:
+              - mediatek,mt8516-musb
+              - mediatek,mt2701-musb
+              - mediatek,mt7623-musb
+          - const: mediatek,mtk-musb
+      - const: mediatek,mt6797-musb
 
   reg:
     maxItems: 1
@@ -33,17 +35,24 @@
       - const: mc
 
   clocks:
+    minItems: 1
     items:
       - description: The main/core clock
       - description: The system bus clock
       - description: The 48Mhz clock
 
   clock-names:
+    minItems: 1
     items:
       - const: main
       - const: mcu
       - const: univpll
 
+  maximum-speed:
+    enum:
+      - high-speed
+      - full-speed
+
   phys:
     maxItems: 1
 
@@ -80,6 +89,27 @@
   - clocks
   - clock-names
 
+allOf:
+  - if:
+      properties:
+        compatible:
+          contains:
+            const: mediatek,mt6797-musb
+    then:
+      # This instance is the musbfsh variant and has only the infra "icusb"
+      # bus gate; there are no separate mcu/univpll gates on this SoC.
+      properties:
+        clocks:
+          maxItems: 1
+        clock-names:
+          maxItems: 1
+    else:
+      properties:
+        clocks:
+          minItems: 3
+        clock-names:
+          minItems: 3
+
 additionalProperties: false
 
 examples:
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/vendor-prefixes.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/vendor-prefixes.yaml
@@ -989,6 +989,8 @@
     description: Open Network Install Environment group
   "^onion,.*":
     description: Onion Corporation
+  "^onsemi,.*":
+    description: onsemi (formerly ON Semiconductor)
   "^onnn,.*":
     description: ON Semiconductor Corp.
   "^ontat,.*":
